  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 103 AM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nA mostly dry Sunday is in the cards with highs climbing into \n60s areawide, but some may call this the calm before the storm. \nMoving into Sunday night, a longwave trough over the western \nCONUS propagates into the Rockies, deepening low pressure along \nthe Front Range of Colorado and Wyoming. As this mid- latitude \ncyclone moves into the Central Plains, it will begin to turn to \nthe northeast, but disagreements among the global ensembles \nstill exist on rather the system tracks through the \nDakotas/Minnesota or through Iowa. This will have major \nimplications on the location of the warm sector, our rainfall \npotential, and even severe weather chances. Latest LREF guidance\nhas PWATs across the CWA climbing near or above 1.5 inches \n(50-70% chance), close to the maximum of climatological values \nfor the first week of November! Regardless of track though, PoPs\nstart to increase on Monday afternoon and stick around into \nWednesday night. General consensus among the ensembles favor a \ncold frontal passage between Tuesday night into Wednesday with \nnorthwesterly flow pumping in some cooler, more seasonable air \nfor the end of next week.\n\nOverall, there is great uncertainty in rainfall totals with \nthis system. The latest LREF guidance shows a 1-inch spread \nbetween the 25th and 75th percentiles with the 01z NBM having a \n60-80% chance of 1+ inches of rainfall through Wednesday \nmorning. One thing is more certain: the winds. Prior to the cold\nfrontal passage, southerly wind gusts of 20-30 mph are likely \n(70-90% chance) Monday afternoon into Wednesday. Gusts after \nWednesday will be dependent on the track of the low pressure. \nRegardless of solution though, a more active pattern compared \nthe last few weeks is in store.",
